{"author":{"id":"85a3c31a64931f83958f26b24045fef8415ea6ff04d942d15661891c77fc0695"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794213117,"nonce":"7hx7ErS9n+z8YvPcWM0JIxkbKY4=","message":"Gate met in the working tree (uncommitted in both repos).\n\nrussl: fixtures/blinc_sdf holds Blinc's sdf_core.wgsl ported to russl as three modules (common: the distance and colour helpers; clip: the aux-data buffer binding, clips, ABI shape clips and gradient tables; sdf_core: the shader, importing both), and `simple`, a #![specialize(super::sdf_core)] variant with Blinc's six FEAT_* values switched off. Both build through rustc to every target with no overrides left. The port was mechanical (a scratch WGSL-to-russl rewrite, then russl's own diagnostics), and found two compiler gaps fixed on the way: textures, derivatives and discard!() (see 8fad30d), and a returning function's tail if/match not returning its branch values.\n\nBlinc: blinc_gpu takes the fixture as a dev-dependency (owner's choice; cargo strips version-less dev-dependencies on publish, so production code and publishing are unchanged). A unit test, russl_parity, builds CoreSimple both ways, from sdf_core.wgsl with SIMPLE_CORE_CONSTANTS through wgpu and from russl's WGSL with none, draws four simple-core primitives (rounded rect, bordered rect, circle, rounded clip) at 128x128, and compares the read-back: every pixel identical on Metal (M-series Mac). The test fails on a quarter-pixel shift of one primitive (168 pixels differ), so it is sensitive. It skips without an adapter that has vertex storage.\n\nOne difference by construction: Blinc passes SIMPLE_CORE_CONSTANTS to the fragment stage only, the vertex stage keeps the defaults; russl's variant fixes them for both stages. For simple-core primitives the vertex output is the same (has_3d is false).\n\nnaga's process_overrides substitutes the values as plain consts but keeps the dead `if false` blocks; the driver compiler removes them, as it does for wgpu's runtime path today.","files":null}]}